// Regression case for commit fc46e9db: an invalid string-literal escape must
// be a parse error, exactly as Cedar rejects it.
//
// Cedar rejects `"a\qb"` with `the input \q is not a valid escape`. Before the
// fix, Dogwood's hand-rolled decode_string accepted ANY unknown escape,
// passing `\q` through verbatim as the two characters `\`, `q`. There was no
// error path at all, so this policy parsed and validated cleanly against a
// real schema — a silent divergence from Cedar. The schema below supplies a
// real `Read` action so this case is rejected ONLY by the escape check: on the
// pre-fix parser it is accepted (the bug), and the expected-failure harness
// fails; after the fix it is a parse error.
permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when { context.input.document == "a\qb" };
